ATTRIBUTION FOR ADAPTED MATERIAL
================================

Wikipedia: Signs of AI writing, and WikiProject AI Cleanup
----------------------------------------------------------
Source:   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Signs_of_AI_writing
Authors:  Wikipedia contributors
Licence:  CC BY-SA 4.0
Retrieved: 2026-07-27

Adapted for: the marker taxonomy, the words-to-watch lists, the
ineffective-indicators list, the vendor residue token table, and the citation
defect categories. Adaptations remain under CC BY-SA 4.0.

The central doctrine of this project is quoted from that guide:

  "The patterns listed here are also only potential signs of a problem, not
  the problem itself. Please do not merely treat these signs as the problems
  to be fixed; that could just make detection harder."


THIRD-PARTY WORK REFERENCED BUT NOT COPIED
==========================================

blader/humanizer
----------------
Source:  https://github.com/blader/humanizer
Licence: MIT
Version: 2.9.1 at 2026-07-22

Analysed as prior art. No code or prose was copied. This project is
deliberately scoped against it: humanizer removes the surface signs of AI
writing, while this project targets the substance defects underneath. The
comparison is documented in anti-slop-brain/references/market-research.md.

impeccable plugin, by Paul Bakaus
---------------------------------
Licence: Apache 2.0

The two-tier detection methodology, first-order lexical and second-order
structural, reached this project by way of a sibling project rather than
directly. Credited because the lineage is real.

Claude Blog Brain, wiki substance scorer
----------------------------------------
anti-slop-brain/scripts/score_substance.py is a port and generalisation of the
check_wiki_substance and score_wiki_substance functions from a sibling project
by the same author. Thresholds were retained unchanged. Credited in the module
docstring.

claude-blog prose linter
------------------------
anti-slop-brain/scripts/lint_voice.py wraps rather than reimplements the
fence-aware and backtick-aware dash detection logic from a sibling project by
the same author.


